```


Here,


/qdgusthrmlirprls


mapped to


qdgusthrmlirprls.php


in the public directory. The first POST ran the command and returned 200. The later POST was the module's cleanup request; the GET independently checked the endpoint. Both received 404 after the file had deleted itself.


This gives defenders a sequence to investigate: public session creation followed by a request to a new, unfamiliar endpoint, potentially followed by 404s for the same path. Session creation alone is normal Remote Support activity. A 200 followed by a 404 is also insufficient by itself; correlate the requests with file changes, session records, and any unusual outbound connections.


### The URL may not contain .php


The earlier PHP canary produced these lines:


```text
192.168.1.68 [28/Oct/2026:20:50:07 +0000] "GET /msfcanary03d80193f0880b.php HTTP/1.1" [0s] [Stat:404] [Size:18] [Ref:-] [UA:Python-urllib/3.9]
192.168.1.68 [28/Oct/2026:20:50:35 +0000] "GET /msfcanary03d80193f0880b HTTP/1.1" [0s] [Stat:200] [Size:368] [Ref:-] [UA:Python-urllib/3.9]
192.168.1.68 [28/Oct/2026:20:50:35 +0000] "GET /msfcanary03d80193f0880b HTTP/1.1" [0s] [Stat:404] [Size:18] [Ref:-] [UA:Python-urllib/3.9]
```


The direct


.php


request failed, while the extensionless request executed the same file. The final 404 followed self-deletion. Detection that only searches for requests ending in


.php


would miss the successful request in this example.


The native transfer itself travels inside the customer MRS connection. These access-log entries show the HTTP trigger, not the file contents or the forged router ID. The captured HTTP log does not expose the inner MRS messages, so it cannot establish the origin bypass on its own.


### Files and connections


Look for unexpected PHP files under the site's public directory and receive files ending in


.php.n$t


. An interrupted transfer may leave the temporary file even if the final URL returns 404. The


.n$t


suffix also belongs to the normal transfer implementation, so its presence needs context.


### Native file-transfer traces


thin-scc.log


and


thin-scc-errors.log


contain more direct evidence of the file-service requests. For example, this line from


thin-scc-errors.log


names the action, native provider, and intended PHP destination. Only


ckey


and


downloadToken


have been redacted:


```text
20261029 17:01:33 999999 thin-scc 11801:proc{LocalFileTransfer}(t11832) FT:WARNING>DISCARDING NONLOCAL MESSAGE: LocalFileTransfer:action=DownloadAnonymousFiles;ckey=<REDACTED>;downloadToken=<REDACTED>;enableResume=0;ftsName=local;path=/home/msfuser/ingredi-24.1.2/app/www/public/envresolve28f788af6a1c39bf.php;serverAddress=1:1022182175:9000;wantOverwritePrompts=0
```


This was our negative control: the request used the customer's ordinary source address and was rejected. It is evidence of an attempted invocation, not a successful upload. The forged local source passes the check that produces this warning, so a successful exploit need not leave a


DISCARDING NONLOCAL MESSAGE


entry.


The successful PHP module run left these lines in


thin-scc.log


:


```text
20261029 17:06:37    319 thin-scc 14419:proc{LocalFileTransfer}(t14486) MRS_STREAM:INFO>createStream: connecting to address 1:1129726787:1031
20261029 17:06:37      2 thin-scc 14419:proc{LocalFileTransfer}(t14486) MRS_CL:ERROR>nodeID not found in node list even after renumerating: 0
20261029 17:06:37      0 thin-scc 14419:proc{LocalFileTransfer}(t14486) MSG_DSP:ERROR>failed to create a mailbox for sending to: 0:0:0
```


Our requests omit


responseAddress


. The handler starts the transfer, then tries to send its action response to an empty address. That produces the


0:0:0


error without preventing the file write. The payload callback independently confirms execution at


17:06:37


.


Look for this combination in the


proc{LocalFileTransfer}


context, especially around unfamiliar PHP requests or outbound connections. The


1:1129726787:1031


value identifies an internal MRS mailbox; use the brain connection records below for the client IP. These entries do not identify the written filename or prove execution on their own. The empty-address errors also depend on how the exploit request is constructed; another implementation may leave different traces.


