brain.log


helps link the activity to a client address. These two entries preceded the same module run:


```text
20261029 17:06:30      0 brain 6339:lstner(t06500) BRN_LSTN:INFO>received fd: 47 app name: 'ingredi support desk customer' and remoteIPAddress: '192.168.1.68' and localIPAddress: '192.168.1.98' and isLoopback '0'
20261029 17:06:30      0 brain 6339:lstner(t06500) BRN_LSTN:INFO>received fd: 49 app name: 'ingredi support desk customer' and remoteIPAddress: '192.168.1.68' and localIPAddress: '192.168.1.98' and isLoopback '1'
```


These are useful for a timeline, but customer connections are normal activity. We found no


UploadFiles


or


DownloadAnonymousFiles


action records in the captured brain logs. Generic EOF, connection-reset, and conference errors also appeared; they are too broad to treat as exploit indicators by themselves.


### Where to collect the native logs


The aggregated logs are under


$BG_app_root/log/


. While a worker is running, its own logs are under


$BG_app_root/data/tmp/sdcust_thin_client/<thinMint>/


. The wrapper appends those logs to the site's log directory when the worker exits, then clears the per-worker copies. Collect both locations when investigating a live session.


The captured


brain.ini


and


thin-scc.ini


both had the debug output disabled with


enabled=0


. No debug log was captured, and we did not change the logging configuration. Brain's debug configuration also limits


MRS_*


categories to


Info


, so enabling its debug output alone would not guarantee detailed message payloads.


Names such as


UploadFileStarted


and


DownloadFileEnded


occur in the native protocol's callback code, but we did not find them in the collected logs. They should not be presented as default audit events
